---
title: Social login
description: Letting users sign in with Google or GitHub.
section: Configuring a tenant
---

External identity providers are configured **per OAuth client**, on its **Providers** tab. Two
kinds are supported today: **Google** and **GitHub**.

## How it fits together

GateKeeper stays the identity provider your application talks to. Google or GitHub is upstream of
that — GateKeeper delegates the *authentication* step, then issues its own tokens as usual.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant U as User
    participant App as Your app
    participant GK as GateKeeper
    participant G as Google

    U->>App: "Sign in with Google"
    App->>GK: /oauth/authorize
    GK->>G: redirect to Google
    U->>G: authenticate
    G-->>GK: callback with Google's code
    GK->>GK: link or create the user
    GK-->>App: GateKeeper's own tokens
```

Your application does not integrate with Google. It integrates with GateKeeper, and GateKeeper
integrates with Google. Adding a provider later changes nothing in your app.

## Setting one up

You will need an OAuth app registered with the upstream provider first (Google Cloud Console, or
GitHub Developer Settings). From it you get a client id and a client secret, which you paste into
the Providers tab.

The redirect URI you register **with Google or GitHub** is GateKeeper's callback, not your app's:

```text
https://gatekeeper.example.com/v1/auth/oauth-provider/google/callback
```

> [!TIP]
> A common first mistake is registering *your application's* callback with Google. The user is
> coming back to GateKeeper, not to you — your app's redirect URI is a separate thing, configured
> on the client's Overview tab.

## Account linking

When someone signs in with Google and the email matches an existing user in the tenant, the social
identity is linked to that user. They now have two ways into one account, rather than two accounts.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> This means the security of the account is only as good as the upstream provider's. If a user's
> Google account is compromised, so is their GateKeeper account. This is the accepted trade-off of
> social login — it is worth being deliberate about which apps you enable it for.

Provider client secrets are encrypted at rest, not stored in plaintext.
